# Derivation

The [partial logic](partial-logic.hmd) gives each atom a status and compares
atoms only when they are the same string. This document adds the step that
makes it a formal system: rules of inference over the atoms, and a script
that applies them. The concept is defined by the card `formal-system` in the
knowledge base. The reading of the rules as distances, and their proofs, are
in [from distance to logic](distance-to-logic.hmd).

## The four parts

| Part | In this vault |
|---|---|
| Language | the atoms `predicate(first, second)` over the fourteen predicates |
| Axioms | the atoms that some claim asserts, each with the address of its claim |
| Rules of inference | the list under `metadata.rules` in the header of the card `knowledge-graph` |
| Theorems | the atoms the rules yield from the axioms in finitely many steps |

A denied atom is not an axiom and is never a premise. It takes part at the
end, when a derived atom is compared with it.

"Proved" here means derived from named claims by named rules. It does not
mean true. Most axioms are graded `argued`, and a derivation passes their
uncertainty on to its conclusion.

## The rules

Rules are data, held beside the schema of the graph. Each is a list of atoms
with the variables `x`, `y` and `z`, and either the atom they give or the
statement that they clash. Two variables of one rule never take the same
value.

| Rule | From | Gives | Why it holds |
|---|---|---|---|
| `is-a-chain` | `is-a(x, y)`, `is-a(y, z)` | `is-a(x, z)` | every x is a y and every y is a z |
| `instance-up` | `instance-of(x, y)`, `is-a(y, z)` | `instance-of(x, z)` | a member of a class is a member of every wider class |
| `requires-chain` | `requires(x, y)`, `requires(y, z)` | `requires(x, z)` | what is needed by something needed is needed |
| `suffices-chain` | `suffices-for(x, y)`, `suffices-for(y, z)` | `suffices-for(x, z)` | what is enough for something that is enough is enough |
| `part-of-chain` | `part-of(x, y)`, `part-of(y, z)` | `part-of(x, z)` | a part of a part is a part |
| `precedes-chain` | `precedes(x, y)`, `precedes(y, z)` | `precedes(x, z)` | order is passed along |
| `same-is-a` | `same-as(x, y)` | `is-a(x, y)`, in both directions | the same concept is a kind of itself under its other name |
| `same-chain` | `same-as(x, y)`, `same-as(y, z)` | `same-as(x, z)` | two things the same as a third are the same |
| `precedes-one-way` | `precedes(x, y)`, `precedes(y, x)` | a clash | nothing comes before what comes before it |

The chain rules for `is-a`, `requires` and `suffices-for`, and the two rules
for `same-as`, are the transitivity of entailment and of equivalence, proved
in [from distance to logic](distance-to-logic.hmd). `part-of-chain` and
`precedes-chain` are the two further transitive predicates that document
names. `instance-up` is a rule between two predicates and rests on the
readings of `instance-of` and `is-a` alone.

A symmetric predicate is matched in both orders of its arguments, and what a
rule gives is written back in alphabetical order.

### A rule that was tried and left out

`generalizes(x, y)` gives `is-a(y, x)` reads "a wider form of" as class
inclusion. Applied to the vault it gave four atoms, and two of them are
wrong:

```
generalizes(diagonal-lemma, godel-sentence)      gives   is-a(godel-sentence, diagonal-lemma)
generalizes(render-integrate-execute, observer-card-experiment)
                                                 gives   is-a(observer-card-experiment, render-integrate-execute)
```

A Gödel sentence is a sentence the diagonal lemma produces. It is not a kind
of lemma. The cards use `generalizes` for "is the general construction
behind" as well as for "is the wider class of", and only the second is
inclusion. The rule stays out until the two uses are told apart in the atoms.
This bears on the table of readings in
[from distance to logic](distance-to-logic.hmd), which places `generalizes`
in the distance of `is-a`.

A rule earns its place the way this one lost it: apply it, read every atom it
gives, and keep it only if none of them is false.

## Cost and belief

Every derivation has a cost, as [from distance to logic](distance-to-logic.hmd)
defines it. An axiom believed at `p` costs `−log₂ p` bits, and the costs of
the steps add. An atom that several claims assert is taken at the highest of
their credences. Where an atom has more than one derivation, the cheapest is
kept.

Two beliefs are read off a derivation.

| Number | How it is computed | What it assumes |
|---|---|---|
| `product` | `2^(−cost)`, the product of the beliefs of the axioms used | that the claims hold or fail independently |
| `bound` | one minus the sum of the doubts of the axioms used, never below 0 | nothing |

The claims of this vault come from one line of discussion and are not
independent, so `product` is an estimate and `bound` is the number that can
be defended. Both are computed from credences that are still the priors of
their evidence grades.

## Contradictions

A derived atom that some claim denies has the status `both`, and its address
is the derivation together with the denial. A match of a rule that declares a
clash is reported in the same way. Nothing is concluded from either: a
contradiction stays a fact about the claims it names.

A derivation may start from an axiom that another claim denies. It is still
given, and the contested axiom is named beside it.

## Running it

```
python3 scripts/derive.py                        summary and all sections
python3 scripts/derive.py --contradictions       derived contradictions and clashes
python3 scripts/derive.py --derived              atoms that follow and no claim states
python3 scripts/derive.py --prove "is-a(a, b)"   the derivation of one atom
```

`--prove` prints the derivation down to the claims and exits with a failure
when the atom is not derivable.

```
is-a(simplification, cognitive-operation)   by is-a-chain
  is-a(simplification, typed-transformation)   asserted by simplification#7 at 0.6
  is-a(typed-transformation, cognitive-operation)   asserted by cognitive-operation#5 at 0.6, typed-transformation#1 at 0.6
cost 1.474 bits, belief 0.360 as a product, at least 0.200
```

The script is Python and reads the card headers directly. The derivation is a
fixed point over the atoms, and it moves into the query library when the
query language can express one.

## What it finds today

- 9 rules, 122 asserted atoms, 85 denied atoms.
- 7 atoms are derived that no claim states.
- No derived atom is denied by a claim, and no rule reports a clash. The one
  contradiction of the vault is still the stated one, on
  `same-as(compression, intelligence)`.

The yield is small, and the reason is in the atoms. Most of them do not
chain: an atom's second argument is seldom another atom's first under the
same predicate. Of the 52 claims that carry a `same-as`, 51 deny it, and a
denial is no premise. The rules are sound and have little to work on. More
will follow from them as the atoms are written with shared arguments, and
that, not a longer list of rules, is where the next gain is.

## Checking a translation

A derivation shows that an atom follows. It does not show that the atom says
what its sentence meant. That step is a translation and is checked in one of
two ways, neither of which is a proof.

- **Back.** The atom is read out as a sentence by its predicate's reading
  and compared with the claim's `text`.
- **Twice.** The claim is put into the logic by two hands independently, and
  the two results are compared. For atoms they must be the same string. For
  richer formulas the comparison is a question for a prover.

Neither check is automated here.

## What this does not do

- It derives no denials. From an asserted `is-a(x, y)` and a denied
  `instance-of(a, y)` nothing is concluded about `instance-of(a, x)`.
- It has no rule between `requires` and `suffices-for`, and none that copies
  the atoms of one thing onto another thing that is the `same-as` it. The
  second would spread any wrongly asserted `same-as` across the vault.
- It does not reach the claims that have no atomic formula, about a third of
  them.
- It does not prove that the cheapest derivation gives the largest distance
  that respects the steps. [From distance to logic](distance-to-logic.hmd)
  says what that proof still needs.
